---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Eric Nolan: technical product management, engineering, trustworthy AI systems, Scouting leadership, and practical building."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Product judgment grounded in engineering.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        My background spans software engineering, performance engineering, enterprise release delivery, and technical product management. I am most useful where a problem needs both product judgment and enough technical depth to challenge how the system will actually behave.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split-feature">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What I optimize for</h2>
        <p>
          Credibility, maintainability, explicit tradeoffs, bounded authority, and systems that fail in understandable ways. I am less interested in maximizing autonomy than in using the right amount of automation for the job.
        </p>
        <p>
          That shows up in AI work as context authority, verification, deterministic controls, and failure handling. It shows up in product work as narrow workflows, non-goals, privacy boundaries, and data models that reflect how people actually behave.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="feature-note">
        <strong>Builder is a useful word here.</strong>
        <p>I build software, workflows, research frameworks, shop fixtures, jigs, furniture, and tools for my own recurring problems. The medium changes; the bias toward useful systems does not.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--warm">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="identity-card">
        <div class="identity-mark" aria-hidden="true">UC</div>
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Why “Unyielding Claw”?</p>
          <h2>It predates the GitHub handle.</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Unyielding Claw is my Mic-O-Say name.</strong> I am a Sachem in the Heart of America Council's Tribe of Mic-O-Say, an honor camping and leadership society within Scouting America. I use the name here because it is a long-standing part of my identity—not because an AI branding exercise produced it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://www.hoac-bsa.org/micosay">Learn about Mic-O-Say from the Heart of America Council →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>Public-safe by design</h2>
      <p>
        This site documents personal projects, public reference implementations, private-project case studies, and general professional experience. Employer code, data, credentials, confidential implementations, and proprietary details are intentionally excluded.
      </p>

      <h2>Evidence over positioning</h2>
      <p>
        I would rather show the decisions inside a real project than stack labels into a headline. The case studies are written around the problem, constraints, tradeoffs, architecture, verification, and what I would change next.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
